From: CosmosEx Buildroot <4372835+planeturban@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:41:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ce_main_app: time sync: take the NTP answer's transmit time

readNtpPacket() set the clock from word 4 of the answer, the reference
timestamp: the moment the server's own clock was last corrected, which is
seconds to many minutes in the past (two answers 20 s apart were 3 s behind
and 11 s ahead of the system clock; the clock ended up 17 s slow). The
transmit time, as the comment above the line says, is word 10. Also refuse
an answer shorter than an NTP packet.
---
 ce_main_app/timesync.cpp | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/ce_main_app/timesync.cpp b/ce_main_app/timesync.cpp
index 16d21638..cdac2e39 100644
--- a/ce_main_app/timesync.cpp
+++ b/ce_main_app/timesync.cpp
@@ -182,10 +182,17 @@ void TimeSync::readNtpPacket(void)
 
     /*
      * The high word of transmit time is the 10th word we get back
+     * (word 4 is the reference timestamp: when the server's own clock was
+     * last set, which can be many minutes ago)
      * tmit is the time in seconds not accounting for network delays which
      * should be way less than a second if this is a local NTP server
      */
-    tmit = ntohl((time_t)buf[4]);    //# get transmit time
+    if(n < 48) {
+        Debug::out(LOG_ERROR, "TimeSync: NTP packet too short (%ld bytes)", (long)n);
+        eState = NTP_FAILED;
+        return;
+    }
+    tmit = ntohl(buf[10]);          //# get transmit time
 
     /*
      * Convert time to unix standard time NTP is number of seconds since 0000
